$DIR/main.p:18:10: cannot print a value of type main.Fd
    An owner cannot be printed, even when its underlying type is an integer:
    printing it would raise the question of whether the value moves. print and
    println write a boolean, an integer, a floating-point number, a string, or
    an address.
    To print the number inside an owner, convert it; the conversion reads the
    value without moving it:
        println(int32(fd))
    To print other values, use pluto/fmt:
        import "pluto/fmt"

        fmt.Println(x)
    (see "Bootstrapping" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2521" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:19:10: cannot print a value of type main.Pair
    print and println write a boolean, an integer (an enum too), a
    floating-point number, a string, or the address of a pointer or function.
    They have no form for other types, so a program that prints them gets the
    same text from every compiler, and they work in a program that has no
    standard library.
    To print a value of another type, use pluto/fmt:
        import "pluto/fmt"

        fmt.Println(x)
    (see "Bootstrapping" in the Plutonium spec, section 13.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2521" for a longer explanation)

